Title: git.md: record that a worktree session cannot run bare git
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T06:40Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

In a worktree-isolated session, every bare `git ...` call is refused. The rtk
PreToolUse hook rewrites `git X` into `rtk git X`; the worktree isolation guard
cannot then prove the command stays inside the worktree, and fails closed:
"runs rtk with a git command among its operands ... what it runs cannot be shown
not to be git".

Both hooks are behaving correctly. The deadlock is emergent, and neither hook's
message names the other, so the failure reads as "git is broken here". Piping or
redirecting does not help, unlike the output-rewrite workaround already recorded
in git.md -- it is the command rewrite the guard objects to, not the framing.

Cost when met cold (git-erg salvage session, 2026-09-16): six refused calls
across three distinct phrasings before the pattern was recognised.

## Relevant files

- `rules/git.md` -- section "Reading state you will act on", after the existing
  rtk bullet, which primes the reader on the same actor

## Actions

1. Add one bullet naming the workaround `/usr/bin/git -C <worktree>`: rtk
   rewrites only the bare token `git`, and the explicit `-C` is the confinement
   proof the guard wants.
2. Note the two adjacent refusals in the same family -- a compound command that
   merely mentions a repo-ish path in a variable assignment, and a `gh` call
   with a `--jq` expression inside a loop -- with the remedy of moving
   multi-step logic into a script file.

## Verification

- [ ] `tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py` passes; headroom was 1427 chars
- [ ] The addition stays well inside that, per the test's own note that the
      headroom "fits a clarifying sentence, not a section"

## Invariants

- No change to either hook in this ticket; this records the interaction.
- The existing rtk bullet stays as-is -- it documents a different failure
  (output rewriting), and merging the two would blur which one is being met.

## Exit criteria

- `rules/git.md` names the deadlock and the `/usr/bin/git -C` workaround
- The resident budget test still passes
